WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.240
(dramatic music)

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:05.690
- [Narrator] The market for
sports trading cards is booming.

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:07.870
- Sports is a lot of people's escape.

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:10.070
Why not invest in a player as opposed

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:11.440
to investing in stock?

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.640
- [Narrator] The top
25 most expensive cards

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.515
in history were sold
in the last two years,

00:00:16.515 --> 00:00:19.300
including an Honus Wagner card sold

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.920
for over $6.6 million.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.840
- A lot of us were holding
on these cards for 30 years,

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:25.810
hoping some day that
they'd be worth something,

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:27.630
hoping some day I could say I told you so.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.820
- [Reporter] Big time
investors like hedge funds

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.300
are also in on it.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.250
They've begun buying trading cards

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:35.310
as alternatives to equities.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.720
- It's culture as an asset,
it's nostalgia as an asset.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.559
- [Narrator] So how did
trading cards balloon

00:00:40.559 --> 00:00:43.270
into a multi-billion dollar industry?

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:45.450
And why are both everyday people

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.941
and big name investors
turning to trading cards

00:00:47.941 --> 00:00:50.050
as investment assets?

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:51.790
This is Niche Markets,

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:54.800
a series that examines
small, lesser-known markets

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.550
that despite their size,
punch well above their weight.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:00.830
- In front of us, we
have a bunch of cards,

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:04.070
one of 'em being as early as the 1934.

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:06.220
A famous card called the Goudey,

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:09.490
all the way down to Honus
Wagner and Joe DiMaggio.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:10.960
We believe that they are authentic.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:12.780
We'll send them in to get graded.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.930
- [Narrator] To verify
a card's authenticity

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:16.610
and determine how much it's worth,

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:19.810
today's card owners rely on
a practice known as grading.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:22.650
Collectors send their
raw or ungraded cards

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.470
to grading agencies where
their cards will be examined

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.490
and assigned a 1 through 10 grade.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:30.090
Card scores are based on
four different factors:

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:34.480
centering, corners, edges and surface.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:35.960
- So we ended up getting them all graded.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:37.096
They all came back real.

00:01:37.096 --> 00:01:39.900
One is the lowest grade you can receive.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.160
That is as terrible as it comes.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.930
There's a crease right down
the entire middle of it,

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:49.010
corners are all peeled up,
edges are all dinged up.

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:52.420
This card's probably around
a 100 to $200 card raw

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:54.150
but once you get it graded, it's real,

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.090
it's authenticated, it's now a one.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:57.410
We now know the value.

00:01:57.410 --> 00:01:59.350
It's around 800 to $1,000.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.980
To know that its value is worth X or Y

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.760
because of its grade is what
really turned this hobby

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.950
into what it is now as a stock market.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.490
- [Narrator] In the past, buyers
had to take a seller's word

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:12.910
on the legitimacy of a card's condition

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:16.000
but today thanks to
grading companies like PSA,

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.840
Beckett and SGC acting as
neutral arbiters of quality,

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.167
a card's price is much easier to verify.

00:02:22.167 --> 00:02:24.170
- One of the things my mom always said

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:25.560
that I couldn't really have an answer for

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.700
was that card's not worth
$20 until you get someone

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.181
to give $20 for it.

00:02:30.181 --> 00:02:32.093
And that's no longer true.

00:02:32.093 --> 00:02:34.730
(pensive music)

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:36.790
- [Narrator] Burt Deveau, Jesse Deveau,

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:38.655
and Jeffrey Ferraro are the owners

00:02:38.655 --> 00:02:41.430
of FRESHPULLZ, a trading card shop located

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:43.340
in the outskirts of Boston.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:45.670
The three friends opened the store in May

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.980
after making a small fortune
auctioning cards online.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:50.670
- We did $2 million in sales.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.240
We should break the $3 million mark

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.723
within the next month
by the end of September.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:57.600
- [Narrator] Since the
trio started their business

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.130
in March of 2020, they've
all left their jobs

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:01.750
in the hospitality industry,

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:05.090
and seen their material
circumstances transformed.

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:06.930
- I used to work 70 hours a week

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:08.810
in a kitchen and be absolutely miserable.

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:10.900
Now I can work 150 hours here

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:14.040
and it doesn't seem like 150 hours.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.350
- [Narrator] FRESHPULLZ's
story is but one example

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:19.050
of card owners striking
it rich in today's market.

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:20.340
Since the pandemic began,

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:23.600
reports of former hobbyists
digging up their old collections

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.630
and discovering a windfall
have not been uncommon.

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:29.537
Sports cards have existed
since the early 1900s

00:03:29.537 --> 00:03:32.010
but in the 1980s and 1990s,

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:34.010
prices for newer cards cratered

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:36.330
after manufacturers flooded the market.

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:39.050
- Ken Griffey Jr., his
rookie card is known

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:41.940
for being one of the
most mass-produced cards,

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:43.860
and it's just not valuable.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:45.520
- [Narrator] The pandemic
also triggered a string

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:48.890
of deals and acquisitions that
have shaken up the industry.

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:51.750
Earlier this year, the investment
firm The Chernin Group,

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:54.450
along with celebrities like
Mark Cuban and Kevin Durant

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:56.260
invested $40 million in one

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:58.490
of the industry's
best-known auction houses,

00:03:58.490 --> 00:03:59.900
Goldin Auctions.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:01.830
This July, Goldin was acquired

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:04.130
by the investment group
Collectors Holdings.

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:06.820
- It's an exciting time for
everybody here at Goldin.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:09.760
It's an exciting time for
the industry as a whole.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:10.940
- [Narrator] Also in July,

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:12.900
Blackstone acquired a majority stake

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.580
in the grading company
Certified Collectibles Group,

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:18.673
which values CGC at
more than $500 million.

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:21.910
- These people have people to tell them

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:23.070
where to invest their money.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.770
They're not telling
them to invest billions

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:28.483
of dollars into the card
market for it to just collapse.

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:32.780
- Right now I'm holding two
of my most favorite cards.

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:36.940
They both are a 1997 Kobe
Bryant Precious Metal Gems.

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:40.080
The red version, there're
100 of them in the world.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:43.220
This is a PSA 5.5, this one's a PSA 4.5.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:45.530
This card right her I bought for $13,000.

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:49.371
It's probably worth
between 160 and $200,000.

00:04:49.371 --> 00:04:51.410
- [Narrator] Leore Alvidar
was an avid card collector

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:52.340
when he was younger,

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:54.270
especially for Kobe Bryant cards

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:57.660
but he didn't return to
the hobby until 2016.

00:04:57.660 --> 00:04:59.210
- I went on eBay,

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:01.320
and the first box of cards that I went

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:03.310
and looked for was the E-X2000.

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:06.456
I had never gotten that
Kobe Bryant green card

00:05:06.456 --> 00:05:09.430
that I really wanted when I was young.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:11.210
And I saw that there were
different price points,

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.200
like some were selling for $30,

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.517
and there was one that
was selling for $3,000.

00:05:15.517 --> 00:05:18.277
And I was like why is one
30 and why is one $3,000?

00:05:20.026 --> 00:05:22.180
- [Narrator] Alvidar, who
has a background in finance,

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:24.750
saw opportunity in the inefficient way

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:26.500
in which pricing was determined.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:28.300
- And I remember seeing
that the probability,

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:29.750
the expected value was positive,

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:32.270
meaning if I kept buying the boxes,

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.990
and probabilistically, I
will get the Kobe Bryant card

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:36.460
and then I sell it on eBay,

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:38.500
I'll make 10 to $20 a box.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:41.620
- [Narrator] After five years
of buying and selling cards,

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:44.200
Alvidar turned his
passion into a business.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:46.620
In March of 2020, he launched Alt,

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:49.280
which aims to be a sort of
Coinbase for collectibles

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:51.440
where users can buy and sell cards,

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:53.920
just like more conventional assets.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.190
Alt brands itself as a
modern financial institution

00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:58.630
that allows people to invest

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:00.160
in things they're passionate about,

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:01.390
just like stocks.

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:02.410
- When you log into Alt,

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:05.530
it feels a lot like Coinbase
or Robinhood or Fidelity.

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:06.960
In real time, you can actually see

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:08.840
how much your portfolio is worth.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:11.840
- [Narrator] Similar to how
Zillow uses its Zestimate figure

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:13.310
to track home prices,

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:16.810
Alt created the Alt Value
to track card prices.

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:18.670
- The number one question
when people start getting

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:21.030
into cards is how much are my cards worth?

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:22.200
The whole point of the Alt Value

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:24.470
is to give people confidence
around the values,

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:26.490
and just increase some of the transparency

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:28.440
that exists in the ecosystem

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:30.670
but just hasn't been brought out

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:33.830
and democratized on the internet so far.

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:35.710
- [Narrator] Alt is just one of a new crop

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:37.390
of digital services catering

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:40.260
to the rapidly growing
trading card market.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:42.270
Collectable and Dibbs allow users

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:45.359
to buy and trade
fractional shares of cards.

00:06:45.359 --> 00:06:48.310
StarStock brands itself as a stock market

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:50.120
for sports cards.

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:52.370
- All right, guys, good
luck, good luck, good luck.

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:54.330
FRESHPULLZ has made most of its money

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:55.870
by hosting a relatively new type

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:58.610
of online auction called Case Breaking.

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:02.120
- Patriot Julian Edelman
and a Kid Reporter.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:02.953
Nothing there to start.

00:07:02.953 --> 00:07:04.880
- [Narrator] Case breaking
is essentially gambling

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:06.650
on unboxing videos.

00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:09.820
A break involves groups of
collectors co-investing in a case

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:11.860
inside of which are packs of cards,

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:15.000
and then watching a
livestream of the unboxing.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.020
- Paton, my boy, Manning, rookie.

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:19.150
Nice card, that's how we start off.

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:21.540
First box, Mojo, baby.

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:24.690
So there's a $2,000 box of NBA Basketball.

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:28.830
10 of us spend $200 and we
each get three random teams.

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:31.200
So let's say you have
the Celtics, the Knicks

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:32.170
and the Wizards.

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:33.540
I open up the box,

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:35.420
every Celtics, Knicks and Wizards cards

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:37.430
in that entire box all goes to you.

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:39.600
- [Narrator] Days later,
collectors receive the cards

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:41.180
they won in the mail.

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:43.070
Two packs left, two packs left.

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:44.470
- One of the things that
people asked us at first,

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:47.590
like why would somebody pay
you to open their cards?

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:49.666
And it's not that they're
paying us to open the cards,

00:07:49.666 --> 00:07:50.640
they're having an experience.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:52.290
If you take the pack home
and open it by yourself

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:53.800
in your bedroom, you're alone.

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:55.200
When you do it with us,
you're in a room full

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:56.220
of your friends.

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:58.550
- In the grand scheme of
things, it's a casino.

00:07:58.550 --> 00:08:00.450
You're coming in here, and gambling.

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:02.740
Most of the people in
this world love sports,

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:06.330
and Fantasy Football, and gambling.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:08.760
Those are the things that
aren't going anywhere.

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:10.570
Joshua, Kelly, Rookie.

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:13.640
Too bad it wasn't a damn Herbie.

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:15.070
I don't see the card market going anywhere

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:17.140
for a long, long time.

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:18.013
Van Jefferson.

00:08:18.891 --> 00:08:22.760
The card, Purple Star.

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:25.198
That is fresh.
- That is dope.

00:08:25.198 --> 00:08:27.865
(pensive music)

